    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d161">

                <pb facs="0249" n="237" xml:id="pg237"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860D.00/1148: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Finland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">McClintock</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Helsinki</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-02-18T13:00:00+02:00">February 18,
                            1943—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 18—12:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>298. Source mentioned in my 297 today<anchor n="92" xml:id="d161-f1"/> said that
                    during conversation with Prime Minister Rangell he was closely questioned as to
                    American peace aims and what we proposed to do after the war was won. Rangell
                    stressed this [<hi rendition="#italic">his</hi>] interest in whether United
                    States could exert effective influence on Soviet Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">McClintock</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d161-f1" n="92" xml:id="d161-f1-content">
                <p>The source cited in this telegram was Albin Johnson, International News Service
                    correspondent in Finland.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
